<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="homework" label="homework" component="homework">
  <title>Homework</title>
  <p>
    Homework assignments are a critical component of the course.
    They are designed to reinforce the concepts covered in lectures and to give you practice writing careful mathematical arguments.
    Homework is neither collected nor graded directly.
    However, the presentation problems for each class meeting are drawn from the assigned reading and homework, and your written proofs of these problems form your <xref ref="portfolio" text="title"/>.
    Collaboration is encouraged, but you should be able to reproduce every solution on your own.
  </p>

  <p component="graduate">
    In addition to the problems assigned to all students, graduate students will be assigned advanced exercises as their presentation problems, in place of those for undergraduates, and these are listed separately in the <xref ref="schedule" text="title"/>.
    These exercises require a deeper understanding of the material and the ability to explore alternative approaches.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Homework will be assigned regularly.
    The problems are chosen to highlight the core concepts from the material.
    Mastery of these homework sets serves as a good indicator for performance on assessments.
    As such, you should ensure that you fully understand the material on these homework sets; that is, upon completion of the homework set, you should be capable of completing similar problems without the aid of the text, a computer, or any other tools not available during an exam.
  </p>
</subsection>
